<section id="why">
  <div class="container">
    
    <div class="section-label">Architecture Decision Record</div>
    <h2>How the methodology was proven before it was named</h2>

    <p style="color:var(--gray); margin-bottom: 2.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem; max-width: 640px;">
      The huhuGERMAN ADR was written after the MVP, not before. The friction events came first.
      The architectural diagnosis followed. That sequence — observe, document the structural cause,
      enforce the contract — is how this engagement works.
    </p>

    <div class="adr-timeline">
      <div class="adr-entry">
        <div class="adr-date">2011 — Origin</div>
        <div class="adr-title">Problem: a method that worked, with no record of why or where it broke</div>
        <div class="adr-desc">
          Structured observation began at FCPyS-UNAM: where in the learning sequence did
          authentic input produce confusion instead of comprehension? The method preceded
          the theory. The observation preceded the system.
          <br/><strong>For your system:</strong> An audit with no longitudinal basis is a snapshot.
          The diagnostic discipline here comes from 15 years of watching the same system surface
          and clarify its own architectural failures.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="adr-entry">
        <div class="adr-date">2022 — MVP</div>
        <div class="adr-title">Problem: the flow worked, but the system was held together by human judgment</div>
        <div class="adr-desc">
          Student submits → AI gives feedback. The pedagogical hypothesis validated.
          But the AI corrected Perfekt for students in week 2 — because context about what
          students had learned lived in the teacher's head, not the system.
          <br/><strong>For your system:</strong> Every team ships before the architecture is tight.
          The question is whether you have documented where human judgment is currently
          holding your onboarding flow together — because that is where the friction lives.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="adr-entry">
        <div class="adr-date">2024 — Friction Discovery</div>
        <div class="adr-title">Problem: user confusion with no architectural explanation in the system</div>
        <div class="adr-desc">
          Students reported confusion from feedback on grammar they had not studied.
          The team's first instinct was to adjust the prompts. The actual diagnosis was
          different: the system had no domain contract defining what the AI was allowed
          to act on. The fix was not a patch — it was a typed schema.
          <br/><strong>For your system:</strong> When your team patches onboarding confusion with
          UI changes and the confusion returns, the patch is treating the symptom.
          The audit finds the missing contract.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="adr-entry">
        <div class="adr-date">2025 — ADR Written</div>
        <div class="adr-title">Decision: formalize the domain contract as the enforced source of truth</div>
        <div class="adr-desc">
          WochenKontextSchema formalizes gelernt / nicht_gelernt / korrektur.
          The prompt-builder constructs every AI prompt from the domain state. Zod validates
          at every boundary. The system cannot generate feedback on concepts the user has not
          encountered — not by convention, but by architecture.
          <br/><strong>For your system:</strong> If your onboarding system's behavior is governed
          by assumptions about user state that are never validated, this is the diagnostic that
          finds the gap and names the contract that closes it.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="adr-entry dim">
        <div class="adr-date">May 2026 — Publication</div>
        <div class="adr-title">The system as evidence in a peer-reviewed research trajectory</div>
        <div class="adr-desc">
          Targeting Die Unterrichtspraxis (AATG · Q3 ESCI). The platform becomes the
          intervention in a documented sequence: B.A. thesis (2015) as theoretical foundation,
          M.Ed. thesis (2020) as diagnostic phase, huhuGERMAN as the architectural solution.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-label" style="margin-top: 5rem;">What This Audit Produces</div>
    <h2 id="pdr">Not a deck. A domain contract with named failure points.</h2>

    <p style="color:var(--gray); margin-bottom: 2.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem; max-width: 640px;">
      The WochenKontextSchema is not a whitepaper. It is a TypeScript type definition
      deployed to a live system, validated at every runtime boundary, and tested against
      real users every trimester. That is the standard of specificity this audit holds itself to.
    </p>

    <div class="adr-timeline">
      <div class="adr-entry">
        <div class="adr-date">The Deliverable</div>
        <div class="adr-title">A structural diagnosis, not a presentation</div>
        <div class="adr-desc">
          The output names the architectural failure points in your onboarding flow —
          where the system's assumptions about user state exceed what the user actually
          knows at that moment. Each finding includes the structural cause and a
          decision record for how to resolve it. Specific enough for an engineering team to act on.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="adr-entry">
        <div class="adr-date">The Standard</div>
        <div class="adr-title">From "I hope users figure it out" to "the system cannot require what the user doesn't have yet"</div>
        <div class="adr-desc">
          The same move that resolved the 2024 friction event in huhuGERMAN is what
          the audit applies to your onboarding architecture: replace implicit assumptions
          about user state with an explicit, validated domain contract.
          The AI obeys. The onboarding flow obeys. The user is not asked to compensate
          for a system that doesn't know where they are.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="adr-entry dim">
        <div class="adr-date">The Scope</div>
        <div class="adr-title">Remote. Project-based. One diagnostic question per engagement.</div>
        <div class="adr-desc">
          Each engagement begins with a scoping call where the activation drop-off is
          mapped against the current analytics instrumentation. The audit then goes one
          layer deeper — into the architecture that your event logs cannot capture.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>